  "sampling": "Each session gives speeches from distinct protocols, and the most recent sessions give one more. Protocols and speeches are ranked by the SHA-256 of the seed and their id, so the draw depends on the seed and the corpus alone.",
  "judgement": "A page passes when its status is 200, it names the speech (Anf. and the number, followed by the speaker as the corpus holds it), and the first 60 characters of every paragraph of the speech occur in its text in order, each after the end of the match before it. Both sides are compared after removing tags, decoding entities and removing all whitespace and invisible formatting characters, because the corpus puts some paragraphs together from several source lines. The page is also judged the same way against the speech before and the speech after it in its protocol, each where the corpus holds one, and a page that passes either of those judgements as well does not pass.",
  "request_limit": 60,
  "requests_made": 50,
  "verdict": "Speeches sampled: 50. Pages that came back with status 200: 50. Of those, pages that name the speech and its speaker: 49; that hold the opening words of the speech's first paragraph: 50; that hold the opening words of every paragraph of the speech in order: 50. Pages judged against at least one neighbouring speech: 49; of these, pages that hold none of their neighbours: 49. Pages that pass every judgement: 49; of these, pages judged against at least one neighbouring speech: 48.",
  "limitation": "One response per address was examined, and only the HTML the server returned; nothing was rendered. An address that failed in a way that may not recur, with a server error, a throttling answer or no status at all, was requested again, up to the number of attempts the run allowed. Only the opening words of each paragraph are compared. A page that drops or changes text inside a paragraph, after its opening words, therefore passes. A paragraph that is missing from the page or out of place goes unnoticed when its opening words occur between the match for the paragraph before it and the opening words of the paragraph after it; for the first paragraph, anywhere earlier on the page; for the last, anywhere later. Only the speeches next to the speech in its protocol are looked for, and only whole: a page that also holds a speech that is not a neighbour, a second copy of the speech, or a neighbour present only in part or headed with another title for its speaker, passes. The server can also answer with the API's XML record of the speech in place of the formatted page; such a page holds the speech's text but has no \"Anf.\" heading, so it fails on the name. How many addresses answer this way is not known. Entries of this run whose page has status 200, holds the opening words of every paragraph of the speech, lacks the name, and holds the elements <anforande_nummer> and <anforandetext>, as the API's XML record does: H109118-59.",
